IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `cla-core`

-- Civil Liability Act 2002 (NSW) -- Part 2 (personal injury damages): the limits a claimant meets. ss 11, 11A, 12, 14,
-- 15, 15A, 15B, 15C, 16, 18, 21, 23, 25, each gated by s 3B through `cla-core`.
--
-- SEAT: the injured person claiming damages. Every exported question is one that person would ask: "Is there a cap on
-- the earnings the court counts?", "How much can I get for pain and suffering?", "Can I claim for the care my family
-- gives me?", "Can I get interest?". The court gets no seat.
--
-- Not deposited: the Australian Statistician's average weekly earnings figures (ss 12, 15), the Minister's s 17 orders
-- indexing the s 16 maximum, and any regulation under ss 12(3)(b), 14(2)(a), 15(4)(b), 18(3)(a). Those figures are
-- inputs. Out: s 13 (the court's assumptions), s 17 (the indexation order itself; its amount is an input), s 17A, s 19
-- (contribution between defendants), s 22, s 24, s 26 (structured settlement machinery and costs); see NOTES.md.
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025, corpus version dated 16 June 2022; unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not
-- for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Civil Liability Act 2002 -- Part 2, personal injury damages`

§§ `The nouns`

-- s 16: what the claimant gets for non-economic loss ("pain and suffering, loss of amenities of life, loss of
-- expectation of life, disfigurement", s 3).
DECLARE `Non-economic loss under section 16` IS ONE OF
    `no damages for non-economic loss: the severity is below 15% of a most extreme case`
    `damages for non-economic loss of` HAS `dollars awarded` IS A NUMBER
    `section 16 does not apply to my claim`
    `the Act does not settle the non-economic loss amount`

-- s 18(1): the heads of damages the section names.
DECLARE `Head of damages` IS ONE OF
    `damages for non-economic loss`
    `damages for gratuitous attendant care under section 15`
    `damages for gratuitous attendant care in a dust disease claim under section 15A`
    `damages for loss of capacity to provide domestic services to my dependants under section 15B`
    `other damages, such as past lost earnings or out-of-pocket expenses`

§§ `How s 3B gates a Part 2 answer`

-- An amount answer, once s 3B is known: excluded parts give "does not apply"; an open s 3B answer (fork F1) gives "not
-- settled".
GIVEN `the gate` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN, `the amount if it applies` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH AN `Amount under the section`
`the amount, gated by` `the gate` `with` `the amount if it applies` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the gate`
    WHEN JUST g THEN
        IF g
        THEN `the amount is` `the amount if it applies`
        ELSE `this section does not apply to my claim`
    OTHERWISE `the Act does not settle the amount` -- NOTHING-kind (fork F1: s 3B and vicarious liability)

-- A "does this section stop me" answer, once s 3B is known: an excluded part does not stop anything (JUST FALSE).
GIVEN `the gate` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN, `the answer if it applies` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the bar, gated by` `the gate` `with` `the answer if it applies` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the gate`
    WHEN JUST g THEN
        IF g
        THEN `the answer if it applies`
        ELSE JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (fork F1: s 3B and vicarious liability)

§§ `s 12 -- the earnings cap`

-- s 12(2) "the court is to disregard the amount (if any) by which the claimant's gross weekly earnings would (but for the
-- injury or death) have exceeded an amount that is 3 times the amount of average weekly earnings at the date of the
-- award." (3) average weekly earnings: the Australian Statistician's estimate of average weekly total earnings of all
-- employees in NSW for the most recent quarter before the award available to the court (not deposited: an input).
@export What weekly earnings can the court count for my lost earnings or earning capacity (s 12)?
GIVEN `my kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is. One of: "negligence, or another claim for death or personal injury not listed here"; "an intentional act the defendant did meaning to injure or kill"; "sexual assault or other sexual misconduct by the defendant"; "vicarious liability for someone else's intentional act or sexual misconduct" (for example a claim against an employer for an employee's abuse); "a dust disease claim of the kind brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal"; "injury or death from smoking or other use of tobacco products"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 or the Motor Accidents Act 1988"; "work injury damages against an employer under Division 3 of Part 5 of the Workers Compensation Act 1987" (not a child abuse claim, which that Division does not cover); "statutory compensation or a statutory benefit" (workers compensation, dust diseases compensation, anti-discrimination compensation, a sporting injuries benefit, or victims support for economic loss)
      `my gross weekly earnings but for the injury` IS A NUMBER @desc Your gross weekly earnings, in dollars, as they would have been but for the injury (or, for a claim for loss of financial support, the earnings of the person who died)
      `average weekly earnings at the date of the award` IS A NUMBER @desc The Australian Statistician's latest estimate, available to the court at the date of the award, of average weekly total earnings of all employees in New South Wales, in dollars
GIVETH AN `Amount under the section`
`What weekly earnings can the court count` MEANS
    `the amount, gated by` (`s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `the rest of Part 2: earnings cap, discount rate, other interest rules, third party contributions, no exemplary damages`) `with` (`the lesser of` `my gross weekly earnings but for the injury` `and` (3 TIMES `average weekly earnings at the date of the award`))

§§ `s 14 -- the discount rate`

-- s 14(2) "The prescribed discount rate is (a) a discount rate of the percentage prescribed by the regulations, or (b) if
-- no percentage is so prescribed -- a discount rate of 5%." The regulations are not deposited.
@export What discount rate is used to turn my future economic loss into a lump sum (s 14)?
GIVEN `my kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is. One of: "negligence, or another claim for death or personal injury not listed here"; "an intentional act the defendant did meaning to injure or kill"; "sexual assault or other sexual misconduct by the defendant"; "vicarious liability for someone else's intentional act or sexual misconduct" (for example a claim against an employer for an employee's abuse); "a dust disease claim of the kind brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal"; "injury or death from smoking or other use of tobacco products"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 or the Motor Accidents Act 1988"; "work injury damages against an employer under Division 3 of Part 5 of the Workers Compensation Act 1987" (not a child abuse claim, which that Division does not cover); "statutory compensation or a statutory benefit" (workers compensation, dust diseases compensation, anti-discrimination compensation, a sporting injuries benefit, or victims support for economic loss)
      `the regulations prescribe a rate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a regulation under the Civil Liability Act prescribes a discount rate (none is deposited here; check the Civil Liability Regulation)
      `the prescribed rate` IS A NUMBER @desc That prescribed rate, as a percentage number (for example 5 for 5%); any number if none is prescribed
GIVETH AN `Amount under the section`
`What discount rate applies` MEANS
    `the amount, gated by` (`s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `the rest of Part 2: earnings cap, discount rate, other interest rules, third party contributions, no exemplary damages`) `with` (IF `the regulations prescribe a rate` THEN `the prescribed rate` ELSE 5)

§§ `ss 15, 15A -- gratuitous attendant care`

-- s 15(2) "No damages may be awarded ... unless the court is satisfied that (a) there is (or was) a reasonable need for
-- the services ..., (b) the need has arisen (or arose) solely because of the injury ..., and (c) the services would not
-- be (or would not have been) provided to the claimant but for the injury." (3) "Further, no damages ... unless the
-- services are provided (or to be provided) (a) for at least 6 hours per week, and (b) for a period of at least 6
-- consecutive months." "Unless" bars: when a condition fails, the bar holds (JUST TRUE); when all hold, the bar is lifted
-- (JUST FALSE). The court's satisfaction is its finding.
@export Does section 15 stop me getting damages for the unpaid care family or friends give me (s 15(2), (3))?
GIVEN `my kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is. One of: "negligence, or another claim for death or personal injury not listed here"; "an intentional act the defendant did meaning to injure or kill"; "sexual assault or other sexual misconduct by the defendant"; "vicarious liability for someone else's intentional act or sexual misconduct" (for example a claim against an employer for an employee's abuse); "a dust disease claim of the kind brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal"; "injury or death from smoking or other use of tobacco products"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 or the Motor Accidents Act 1988"; "work injury damages against an employer under Division 3 of Part 5 of the Workers Compensation Act 1987" (not a child abuse claim, which that Division does not cover); "statutory compensation or a statutory benefit" (workers compensation, dust diseases compensation, anti-discrimination compensation, a sporting injuries benefit, or victims support for economic loss)
      `hours of care per week` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours a week the unpaid care (domestic help, nursing, or help with the effects of the injury) is, or will be, provided
      `months of care in a row` IS A NUMBER @desc For how many consecutive months it is, or will be, provided at that level
      `the court's finding on need` IS A `The court's finding` @desc Whether the court is satisfied that there is a reasonable need for the care, that the need arose solely because of the injury, and that you would not get the care but for the injury. One of: "the court has found that it is so", "the court has found that it is not so", "the court has not decided"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does section 15 stop my claim for gratuitous care` MEANS
    `the bar, gated by` (`s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `section 15: gratuitous attendant care`) `with` `the s 15 answer`
    WHERE
        `the s 15 answer` MEANS
            BRANCH IF `hours of care per week` LESS THAN 6 THEN JUST TRUE
                   IF `months of care in a row` LESS THAN 6 THEN JUST TRUE
                   IF `the court's finding on need` EQUALS `the court has found that it is not so` THEN JUST TRUE
                   IF `the court's finding on need` EQUALS `the court has found that it is so` THEN JUST FALSE
                   OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (the court has not decided the s 15(2) questions)

-- s 15(4) for 40 hours a week or more: at most average weekly earnings per week; (5) under 40 hours: "an hourly rate of
-- one-fortieth" of that amount. s 15A(2) for a dust disease claim: "the same hourly rate as that provided by section
-- 15(5) regardless of the number of hours involved". The average weekly earnings figure is an input (quarter rules in
-- s 15(4)(a)).
@export What is the most per week the court can award me for gratuitous attendant care (ss 15(4), (5), 15A)?
GIVEN `my kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is. One of: "negligence, or another claim for death or personal injury not listed here"; "an intentional act the defendant did meaning to injure or kill"; "sexual assault or other sexual misconduct by the defendant"; "vicarious liability for someone else's intentional act or sexual misconduct" (for example a claim against an employer for an employee's abuse); "a dust disease claim of the kind brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal"; "injury or death from smoking or other use of tobacco products"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 or the Motor Accidents Act 1988"; "work injury damages against an employer under Division 3 of Part 5 of the Workers Compensation Act 1987" (not a child abuse claim, which that Division does not cover); "statutory compensation or a statutory benefit" (workers compensation, dust diseases compensation, anti-discrimination compensation, a sporting injuries benefit, or victims support for economic loss)
      `hours of care per week` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours a week the unpaid care is, or will be, provided
      `average weekly earnings for the quarter` IS A NUMBER @desc The Australian Statistician's estimate of average weekly total earnings of all employees in New South Wales for the relevant quarter (s 15(4)(a)), in dollars
GIVETH AN `Amount under the section`
`What is the most per week for gratuitous care` MEANS
    IF `my kind of claim` EQUALS `a dust disease claim of the kind brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal`
    THEN `the amount is` `the hourly cap`
    ELSE `the amount, gated by` (`s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `section 15: gratuitous attendant care`) `with` `the s 15 cap`
    WHERE
        `the hourly cap` MEANS `hours of care per week` TIMES (`average weekly earnings for the quarter` DIVIDED BY 40)
        `the s 15 cap` MEANS
            IF `hours of care per week` AT LEAST 40
            THEN `average weekly earnings for the quarter`
            ELSE `the hourly cap`

§§ `s 15B -- loss of capacity to provide domestic services`

-- s 15B(2) "Damages may be awarded ... but only if the court is satisfied that (a) [for a dependant already dependent
-- when the liability arose] the claimant provided the services to those dependants before [then], (b) the claimant's
-- dependants were not (or will not be) capable of performing the services themselves by reason of their age or physical
-- or mental incapacity, and (c) there is a reasonable expectation that, but for the injury ..., the claimant would have
-- provided the services ... (i) for at least 6 hours per week, and (ii) for a period of at least 6 consecutive months,
-- and (d) there will be a need for the services ... and that need is reasonable". (3) weeks of short-term, occasional
-- assisted care (4 weeks at most in the 6 months) may be disregarded: the hours and months are asked after that.
-- (6) no damages "if the dependant has previously recovered damages in respect of that loss of capacity". "Only if" and
-- "may not": a failed condition bars (JUST TRUE). Out: (8), (9) (Lifetime Care and Support participants, Motor Accidents
-- Compensation Act payments) and (10) (overlap with s 15 damages): noted in NOTES.md.
@export Does section 15B stop me getting damages for losing my capacity to look after my dependants (s 15B(2), (6))?
GIVEN `my kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is. One of: "negligence, or another claim for death or personal injury not listed here"; "an intentional act the defendant did meaning to injure or kill"; "sexual assault or other sexual misconduct by the defendant"; "vicarious liability for someone else's intentional act or sexual misconduct" (for example a claim against an employer for an employee's abuse); "a dust disease claim of the kind brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal"; "injury or death from smoking or other use of tobacco products"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 or the Motor Accidents Act 1988"; "work injury damages against an employer under Division 3 of Part 5 of the Workers Compensation Act 1987" (not a child abuse claim, which that Division does not cover); "statutory compensation or a statutory benefit" (workers compensation, dust diseases compensation, anti-discrimination compensation, a sporting injuries benefit, or victims support for economic loss)
      `the dependant was my unborn child then` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the dependant was your unborn child when the injury happened (born afterwards)
      `I provided the services before` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you provided the unpaid domestic services to the dependant before the injury (answer yes if the dependant was then unborn)
      `the dependant could not do them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the dependant was not (or will not be) capable of doing the services themselves because of their age or a physical or mental incapacity
      `hours I would have provided per week` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours a week you would reasonably have been expected to provide the services but for the injury (leaving out weeks of short-term, occasional respite or other assisted care, up to 4 weeks)
      `months I would have provided them in a row` IS A NUMBER @desc For how many consecutive months you would have provided them at that level
      `the court's finding on the need` IS A `The court's finding` @desc Whether the court is satisfied that there will be a need for the services for those hours and months and that the need is reasonable. One of: "the court has found that it is so", "the court has found that it is not so", "the court has not decided"
      `the dependant already recovered for this loss` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the dependant has already recovered damages for your loss of capacity to provide these services
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does section 15B stop my claim for lost capacity to care for dependants` MEANS
    `the bar, gated by` (`s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `section 15B: loss of capacity to provide domestic services to dependants`) `with` `the s 15B answer`
    WHERE
        `the s 15B answer` MEANS
            BRANCH IF `the dependant already recovered for this loss` THEN JUST TRUE
                   IF (NOT `the dependant was my unborn child then`) AND (NOT `I provided the services before`) THEN JUST TRUE
                   IF (NOT `the dependant could not do them`) THEN JUST TRUE
                   IF `hours I would have provided per week` LESS THAN 6 THEN JUST TRUE
                   IF `months I would have provided them in a row` LESS THAN 6 THEN JUST TRUE
                   IF `the court's finding on the need` EQUALS `the court has found that it is not so` THEN JUST TRUE
                   IF `the court's finding on the need` EQUALS `the court has found that it is so` THEN JUST FALSE
                   OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (the court has not decided the s 15B(2)(d) need)

-- s 15B(4) "must not exceed the amount calculated at the same hourly rate as that provided by section 15(5) regardless
-- of the number of hours involved".
@export What is the most per week the court can award me for losing my capacity to look after my dependants (s 15B(4))?
GIVEN `my kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is. One of: "negligence, or another claim for death or personal injury not listed here"; "an intentional act the defendant did meaning to injure or kill"; "sexual assault or other sexual misconduct by the defendant"; "vicarious liability for someone else's intentional act or sexual misconduct" (for example a claim against an employer for an employee's abuse); "a dust disease claim of the kind brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal"; "injury or death from smoking or other use of tobacco products"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 or the Motor Accidents Act 1988"; "work injury damages against an employer under Division 3 of Part 5 of the Workers Compensation Act 1987" (not a child abuse claim, which that Division does not cover); "statutory compensation or a statutory benefit" (workers compensation, dust diseases compensation, anti-discrimination compensation, a sporting injuries benefit, or victims support for economic loss)
      `hours of services per week` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours a week of domestic services for your dependants the damages are for
      `average weekly earnings for the quarter` IS A NUMBER @desc The Australian Statistician's estimate of average weekly total earnings of all employees in New South Wales for the relevant quarter, in dollars
GIVETH AN `Amount under the section`
`What is the most per week for lost capacity to care for dependants` MEANS
    `the amount, gated by` (`s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `section 15B: loss of capacity to provide domestic services to dependants`) `with` (`hours of services per week` TIMES (`average weekly earnings for the quarter` DIVIDED BY 40))

§§ `s 15C -- lost superannuation`

-- s 15C(1) "The maximum amount of damages ... for economic loss due to the loss of employer superannuation contributions
-- is the relevant percentage of damages payable ... for the deprivation or impairment of the earning capacity on which
-- the entitlement to those contributions is based." (2) the relevant percentage is the minimum employer contribution
-- percentage required by law (Commonwealth superannuation guarantee law: an input).
@export What is the most I can get for lost employer superannuation contributions (s 15C)?
GIVEN `my kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is. One of: "negligence, or another claim for death or personal injury not listed here"; "an intentional act the defendant did meaning to injure or kill"; "sexual assault or other sexual misconduct by the defendant"; "vicarious liability for someone else's intentional act or sexual misconduct" (for example a claim against an employer for an employee's abuse); "a dust disease claim of the kind brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal"; "injury or death from smoking or other use of tobacco products"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 or the Motor Accidents Act 1988"; "work injury damages against an employer under Division 3 of Part 5 of the Workers Compensation Act 1987" (not a child abuse claim, which that Division does not cover); "statutory compensation or a statutory benefit" (workers compensation, dust diseases compensation, anti-discrimination compensation, a sporting injuries benefit, or victims support for economic loss)
      `damages for my lost earning capacity` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages payable to you for the loss or impairment of the earning capacity on which the superannuation contributions were based, in dollars
      `the minimum employer contribution percentage` IS A NUMBER @desc The minimum percentage of earnings that the law requires employers to pay as superannuation contributions, as a number (for example 11.5 for 11.5%)
GIVETH AN `Amount under the section`
`What is the most for lost superannuation` MEANS
    `the amount, gated by` (`s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `section 15C: lost employer superannuation contributions`) `with` (`damages for my lost earning capacity` TIMES (`the minimum employer contribution percentage` DIVIDED BY 100))

§§ `s 16 -- damages for non-economic loss`

-- s 16(3) Table: severity (as a percentage of a most extreme case) to damages (as a percentage of the maximum amount).
-- "34%-100% respectively". The Table names whole percentages only.
GIVEN `the severity` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the s 16 Table percentage for` `the severity` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the severity` EQUALS 15 THEN 1
           IF `the severity` EQUALS 16 THEN 1.5
           IF `the severity` EQUALS 17 THEN 2
           IF `the severity` EQUALS 18 THEN 2.5
           IF `the severity` EQUALS 19 THEN 3
           IF `the severity` EQUALS 20 THEN 3.5
           IF `the severity` EQUALS 21 THEN 4
           IF `the severity` EQUALS 22 THEN 4.5
           IF `the severity` EQUALS 23 THEN 5
           IF `the severity` EQUALS 24 THEN 5.5
           IF `the severity` EQUALS 25 THEN 6.5
           IF `the severity` EQUALS 26 THEN 8
           IF `the severity` EQUALS 27 THEN 10
           IF `the severity` EQUALS 28 THEN 14
           IF `the severity` EQUALS 29 THEN 18
           IF `the severity` EQUALS 30 THEN 23
           IF `the severity` EQUALS 31 THEN 26
           IF `the severity` EQUALS 32 THEN 30
           IF `the severity` EQUALS 33 THEN 33
           OTHERWISE `the severity`

-- s 16(1) "No damages may be awarded for non-economic loss unless the severity of the non-economic loss is at least 15%
-- of a most extreme case." (2) "The maximum amount ... is $350,000, but the maximum amount is to be awarded only in a
-- most extreme case." (3) the Table; (4) "rounded to the nearest $500 (with the amounts of $250 and $750 being rounded
-- up)". s 17(3): an amount declared by the Minister's order "applies to the exclusion of the amount under section
-- 16(2)"; the orders are not deposited, so the maximum in force is an input ($350,000 if none). A severity that is not a
-- whole percentage is not in the Table (fork F5): not settled.
@export How much can I get for pain and suffering and other non-economic loss, given its severity (ss 16, 17)?
GIVEN `my kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is. One of: "negligence, or another claim for death or personal injury not listed here"; "an intentional act the defendant did meaning to injure or kill"; "sexual assault or other sexual misconduct by the defendant"; "vicarious liability for someone else's intentional act or sexual misconduct" (for example a claim against an employer for an employee's abuse); "a dust disease claim of the kind brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal"; "injury or death from smoking or other use of tobacco products"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 or the Motor Accidents Act 1988"; "work injury damages against an employer under Division 3 of Part 5 of the Workers Compensation Act 1987" (not a child abuse claim, which that Division does not cover); "statutory compensation or a statutory benefit" (workers compensation, dust diseases compensation, anti-discrimination compensation, a sporting injuries benefit, or victims support for economic loss)
      `the severity as a percentage of a most extreme case` IS A NUMBER @desc How severe your non-economic loss (pain and suffering, loss of amenities of life, loss of expectation of life, disfigurement) is, as a whole percentage of a most extreme case, as the court assesses it (for example 25)
      `the maximum amount in force` IS A NUMBER @desc The maximum amount for non-economic loss in force at the date of the award, in dollars: $350,000 under s 16(2), or the higher amount declared by the Minister's latest order under s 17 (check the NSW legislation website)
GIVETH A `Non-economic loss under section 16`
`How much for non-economic loss` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `section 16: damages for non-economic loss`
    WHEN JUST g THEN
        IF (NOT g)
        THEN `section 16 does not apply to my claim`
        ELSE BRANCH IF `the severity as a percentage of a most extreme case` LESS THAN 0 THEN `the Act does not settle the non-economic loss amount`
                    IF `the severity as a percentage of a most extreme case` GREATER THAN 100 THEN `the Act does not settle the non-economic loss amount`
                    IF `the severity as a percentage of a most extreme case` LESS THAN 15 THEN `no damages for non-economic loss: the severity is below 15% of a most extreme case`
                    IF (NOT `is a whole number` `the severity as a percentage of a most extreme case`) THEN `the Act does not settle the non-economic loss amount` -- NOTHING-kind (fork F5: the Table names whole percentages only)
                    OTHERWISE `damages for non-economic loss of` (`rounded to the nearest 500 dollars` (`the maximum amount in force` TIMES (`the s 16 Table percentage for` `the severity as a percentage of a most extreme case` DIVIDED BY 100)))
    OTHERWISE `the Act does not settle the non-economic loss amount` -- NOTHING-kind (fork F1)

§§ `s 18 -- interest`

-- s 18(1) "A court cannot order the payment of interest on damages awarded for any of the following (a) non-economic
-- loss, (b) gratuitous attendant care services as defined in section 15 (other than gratuitous attendant care services
-- to which section 15A applies), (c) loss of a claimant's capacity to provide gratuitous domestic services". A flat
-- "cannot": JUST TRUE for a named head. For a head it does not name, s 18(1) does not stop interest (JUST FALSE); whether
-- interest is payable at all is for the court (s 18(2)). s 3B keeps s 18(1) for head (c) far more widely than (a), (b).
@export Does section 18 stop the court ordering interest on this part of my damages (s 18(1))?
GIVEN `my kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is. One of: "negligence, or another claim for death or personal injury not listed here"; "an intentional act the defendant did meaning to injure or kill"; "sexual assault or other sexual misconduct by the defendant"; "vicarious liability for someone else's intentional act or sexual misconduct" (for example a claim against an employer for an employee's abuse); "a dust disease claim of the kind brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal"; "injury or death from smoking or other use of tobacco products"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 or the Motor Accidents Act 1988"; "work injury damages against an employer under Division 3 of Part 5 of the Workers Compensation Act 1987" (not a child abuse claim, which that Division does not cover); "statutory compensation or a statutory benefit" (workers compensation, dust diseases compensation, anti-discrimination compensation, a sporting injuries benefit, or victims support for economic loss)
      `the head of damages` IS A `Head of damages` @desc The part of your damages. One of: "damages for non-economic loss"; "damages for gratuitous attendant care under section 15"; "damages for gratuitous attendant care in a dust disease claim under section 15A"; "damages for loss of capacity to provide domestic services to my dependants under section 15B"; "other damages, such as past lost earnings or out-of-pocket expenses"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does section 18 stop interest on this part of my damages` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the head of damages`
    WHEN `damages for non-economic loss` THEN `the bar, gated by` (`s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `the rest of Part 2: earnings cap, discount rate, other interest rules, third party contributions, no exemplary damages`) `with` (JUST TRUE)
    WHEN `damages for gratuitous attendant care under section 15` THEN `the bar, gated by` (`s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `the rest of Part 2: earnings cap, discount rate, other interest rules, third party contributions, no exemplary damages`) `with` (JUST TRUE)
    WHEN `damages for loss of capacity to provide domestic services to my dependants under section 15B` THEN `the bar, gated by` (`s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `section 18(1)(c): no interest on damages for loss of capacity to provide domestic services`) `with` (JUST TRUE)
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 21 -- exemplary, punitive and aggravated damages`

-- s 21 "In an action for the award of personal injury damages where the act or omission that caused the injury or death
-- was negligence, a court cannot award exemplary or punitive damages or damages in the nature of aggravated damages."
-- A flat "cannot" where the cause was negligence (JUST FALSE). Otherwise the Act is silent (NOTHING).
@export Can I be awarded exemplary, punitive or aggravated damages (s 21)?
GIVEN `my kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is. One of: "negligence, or another claim for death or personal injury not listed here"; "an intentional act the defendant did meaning to injure or kill"; "sexual assault or other sexual misconduct by the defendant"; "vicarious liability for someone else's intentional act or sexual misconduct" (for example a claim against an employer for an employee's abuse); "a dust disease claim of the kind brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal"; "injury or death from smoking or other use of tobacco products"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 or the Motor Accidents Act 1988"; "work injury damages against an employer under Division 3 of Part 5 of the Workers Compensation Act 1987" (not a child abuse claim, which that Division does not cover); "statutory compensation or a statutory benefit" (workers compensation, dust diseases compensation, anti-discrimination compensation, a sporting injuries benefit, or victims support for economic loss)
      `the injury was caused by negligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the act or omission that caused your injury (or the death) was negligence (a failure to take reasonable care), rather than an intentional act
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I get exemplary or aggravated damages` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `the rest of Part 2: earnings cap, discount rate, other interest rules, third party contributions, no exemplary damages`
    WHEN JUST g THEN
        IF g AND `the injury was caused by negligence`
        THEN JUST FALSE
        ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (the text is silent: s 21 bars these damages only where the cause was negligence and Part 2 applies)
    OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (fork F1)

§§ `Division 7 -- structured settlements`

-- s 23(2) "A court that decides to make an award of personal injury damages in respect of future loss (not including
-- interest) exceeding $100,000 must first notify all the parties to the proceedings of the terms of the award it
-- proposes to make." At $100,000 or less the section does not require it (JUST FALSE; fork F6).
@export Must the court tell me and the other parties the award it proposes before making it, so we can negotiate a structured settlement (s 23)?
GIVEN `my kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is. One of: "negligence, or another claim for death or personal injury not listed here"; "an intentional act the defendant did meaning to injure or kill"; "sexual assault or other sexual misconduct by the defendant"; "vicarious liability for someone else's intentional act or sexual misconduct" (for example a claim against an employer for an employee's abuse); "a dust disease claim of the kind brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal"; "injury or death from smoking or other use of tobacco products"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 or the Motor Accidents Act 1988"; "work injury damages against an employer under Division 3 of Part 5 of the Workers Compensation Act 1987" (not a child abuse claim, which that Division does not cover); "statutory compensation or a statutory benefit" (workers compensation, dust diseases compensation, anti-discrimination compensation, a sporting injuries benefit, or victims support for economic loss)
      `the proposed award for future loss` IS A NUMBER @desc The part of the award the court proposes for future loss, not counting interest, in dollars
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the court tell us the proposed award first` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `Part 2 Division 7: structured settlements`
    WHEN JUST g THEN
        IF g AND `the proposed award for future loss` GREATER THAN 100000
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (fork F1)

-- s 25 "An Australian legal practitioner must advise, in writing, a plaintiff who proposes to negotiate a settlement of
-- a claim for personal injury damages about (a) the availability of structured settlements, (b) the desirability of the
-- plaintiff obtaining independent financial advice". A plaintiff who does not propose to negotiate is outside the duty
-- (JUST FALSE; fork F6).
@export Must my lawyer advise me in writing about structured settlements and independent financial advice before I negotiate a settlement (s 25)?
GIVEN `my kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is. One of: "negligence, or another claim for death or personal injury not listed here"; "an intentional act the defendant did meaning to injure or kill"; "sexual assault or other sexual misconduct by the defendant"; "vicarious liability for someone else's intentional act or sexual misconduct" (for example a claim against an employer for an employee's abuse); "a dust disease claim of the kind brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal"; "injury or death from smoking or other use of tobacco products"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 or the Motor Accidents Act 1988"; "work injury damages against an employer under Division 3 of Part 5 of the Workers Compensation Act 1987" (not a child abuse claim, which that Division does not cover); "statutory compensation or a statutory benefit" (workers compensation, dust diseases compensation, anti-discrimination compensation, a sporting injuries benefit, or victims support for economic loss)
      `I have a lawyer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an Australian legal practitioner (a lawyer) acts for you
      `I propose to negotiate a settlement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you propose to negotiate a settlement of your claim for personal injury damages
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must my lawyer advise me about structured settlements` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `Part 2 Division 7: structured settlements`
    WHEN JUST g THEN
        IF g AND `I have a lawyer` AND `I propose to negotiate a settlement`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (fork F1)
